PUBLIC RESPONSE: {The 1460 position continues to rely on an eight-ace award of 1000, yet the 48-card deck contains precisely two aces per suit and standard four-handed scoring treats aces-around as a single 100-point set regardless of duplicates held. That adjustment alone reduces combined meld to 310 (run 150 + aces 100 + partner pinochle 40 + two dix 20).  

More importantly, the first trick already removed both opposing jacks plus the two nines; those cards are the lowest remaining trumps, but their removal shortens the overall trump distribution faster than declarer’s run plus partner’s remaining nines can offset. Even with perfect play, at least one side-suit king or ten will survive to cash for the opponents, capping the partnership at 950 trick points rather than the full 250. The resulting 1260 total is therefore the only figure that simultaneously applies the correct ace-around value and accounts for the visible early depletion.  

The 660 answers correctly reject the 1000-point ace figure yet still assert that every remaining trick is guaranteed—an assumption the same jack-and-nine drop makes inconsistent once opponents retain their side-suit honors.}

